## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Syntheses and Characterization. Solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3},\ \mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}$ and $\mathsf{N a O H}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ -DMF $({\mathrm{DMF}}\ =$ $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylformamide) yielded colourless block-like crystals of $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})[\mathrm{Eu(L)}]$ (1). SCXRD analysis showed that its asymmetric unit (space group $P_{2_{1}/c})$ consists of one $\mathrm{Eu(III)}$ ion, one $\mathrm{L}^{4^{-}}$ ligand, and one $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ ion (Figure S1) which results from thermal decomposition of the DMF molecule. Each $\mathrm{Eu(III)}$ center is coordinated octahedrally by four carboxylate O atoms of four $\mathrm{L}^{4^{-}}$ ligands and two phosphonate O atoms of two other $\mathrm{L}^{4^{-}}$ ligands. Each $\mathrm{L}^{4^{-}}$ ligand coordinates with six $\mathrm{Eu(III)}$ ions by four $\mathrm{~O~}$ atoms from two carboxylate groups and two O atoms from one phosphate group, leaving one uncoordinated O atom $(\mathrm{O}_{7})$ . This gives rise to a wavy-layered architecture, which can be considered to be composed of countless edge-shared ring units (along the bc plane) with $\mathrm{L}^{4^{-}}$ ligands located in its central regions (Figures 1a and S2). If both $\mathrm{Eu(III)}$ and $\mathrm{L}^{4^{-}}$ are considered as 6-connected nodes, the layered structure can be simplified as a new binodal 6,6-connected network with point symbol $\{4^{15}\}$ .14
The anionic host framework constructed by the interconnection of $\mathrm{[Eu(L)]^{-}}$ units is charge-balanced by the periodically aligned $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ cations that occupy the interlayer region (Figures 1b and $S_{3}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ). Interestingly, a view down the $c$ -axis shows zigzag hydrogen-bonded $(\mathsf{N}-$ $\operatorname{H}{\cdots}\operatorname{O})$ chains each assembled by linking an alternate arrangement of $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ cations and $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{7}}$ atoms of phosphonate groups (Figures 1c and S4). Importantly, the hydrogen-bonded $N{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ groups can be confirmed by two strong and wide IR spectral bands $\nu_{\mathrm{{r}}}$ $(2000~-~2550~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and $\nu_{2}$ $(2550-2800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ (Figure $S_{5}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ),15 which are significantly lower than $2800~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ for normal hydrogen-bonded $\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ stretching vibrations.7f,11,16 Judging from the different distances between donor $\bf(N_{1})$ and acceptor $(\mathrm{O}_{7})$ atoms for H1a $\left({2.693}(2)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right)$ and $\mathrm{H1b}\ (2.716(2)\:\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ , spectral bands $\nu_{\mathrm{{r}}}$ and $\nu_{2}$ can be ascribed to the hydrogen-bonded N–H1a and $_{\mathrm{N-Hub}}$ stretching vibrations, respectively. In addition, it has been demonstrated that the hydrogen-bonded chain retains its integrity in the presence of ammonium analogs (Figure S6 and Table S1). In brief, strongly hydrogen-bonded chains constructed by optimal acid-base pairing has yielded a crystalline material that holds promise as a good proton conductor.11
Prior to proton conductivity measurements, the thermal and water stability of 1 were investigated.17 Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) and differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) showed that 1 can be thermally stable up to 300 $^{\circ}{\cal C}$ (Figures $S_{7}$ and S8), which was also determined by variable-temperature PXRD (Figure 2a). Furthermore, PXRD patterns of samples soaked in water for six months or boiled in water for two weeks did not differ from that of an as-synthesized sample (Figures S9 and S10), indicating that 1 is an excellent thermal- and water-stable crystalline material. In a word, the unique structural characteristics and high thermal and water stabilities of 1 make it a promising candidate as an excellent protonconducting material under both anhydrous and humid conditions.
Proton Conductivities under Anhydrous Conditions. In view of the plentiful strongly hydrogen-bonded chains periodically aligned along the crystallographic $\scriptstyle{c}$ - axis in 1, alternating current (AC) impedance measurements were firstly performed on a selected large singlecrystal with dimensions of about $0.32\ \mathrm{mm}\times\ 0.29\ \mathrm{mm}\times$ $\mathbf{o.26\mm}$ . With the single-crystal orientation determined by PXRD, only one diffraction, indexed as (002), was observed, which identified the $c$ -axis as the orientation direction of the hydrogen-bonded chains (Figure 2b). By attaching gold-pasted electrodes to both identified sides of the single-crystal,13c AC conductivities were measured at $30\mathrm{~-~}150\mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under anhydrous conditions $\mathrm{\bf~N}_{2}$ atmosphere) and the real $(Z)$ and imaginary $(Z^{\prime})$ parts of the impedance spectrum are shown in Figure 2c. The impedance plots could be excellently fitted by a designed equivalent circuit of $R C$ and the tail at low frequencies should correspond to diffusion impedance (Figure S12). At $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the proton conductivity of a single-crystal sample was 1.69 $\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , reaching a maximum of $1.25\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}$ at $\mathbf{150~}^{\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure $\mathrm{S}_{13}\mathrm{\backslash}$ ), which is higher than those of phosphate/sulfonate-based MOFs, such as $\beta\mathrm{-PCMOF}_{2}(\mathrm{Tz})_{\mathrm{o.3}},$ 10g $\mathrm{[Zn(HPO_{4})(H_{2}P O_{4})_{2}]\cdot_{2}H_{2}I m,}$ 18 $[(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{3}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})]_{2}[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathbf{o}$ $\mathbf{x}{\big)}_{3}\mathbf{l}^{\mathrm{u}}$ and $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{TzH})_{2}]$ ,13d and comparable to those of $\left[\mathrm{ImH}_{2}\right]$ $[\mathrm{Cu(H_{2}P O_{4})_{2}C l l}]{\cdot}\mathrm{H_{2}O},$ 19 $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{3}(H_{2}P O_{4})_{6}(H_{2}}}$ $\left|\operatorname{O}\right\rangle_{3}]$ ·Hbim.20 Just as expected, no proton conductivities exist through other crystal faces under similar conditions (Figure $\mathrm{S14}.$ ), which further proves that efficient proton transfer occurs solely along the hydrogen-bonded chains. The proton transfer activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ for singlecrystal of 1 along the $c$ -axis was calculated to be $\mathbf{0.21\eV}$ (Figure 2d) according to the least-square fit of an Arrhenius plot, which is close to the lowest value reported for proton-conductive MOFs under anhydrous conditions (Figure $S_{15}$ and Table S2), and only behind one MOF $[(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{3}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})]_{2}[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ reported by the Ghosh group.11 Such low activation value means that proton conduction in 1 belongs to the Grotthuss (hopping) mechanism $(<0.4~\mathrm{eV})$ , which further supports that the strongly hydrogen-bonded chains constructed by acidbase pairs in 1 can yield facile and efficient proton transfer with the help of electrostatic interactions.11 In contrast, AC impedance measurements of 1 using a compacted pellet of microcrystalline crystals exhibit extremely poor proton conductivity in the range of $\mathbf{10}^{-8}-\mathbf{10}^{-7}\mathbf{\S}{\cdot}\mathbf{cm}^{-1}$ even at 150 $^{\circ}C$ under dry $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere, which is possibly attributable to big grain boundary resistance under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere; in other words, hydrogen-bonded chains cannot link up cooperatively among microcrystalline crystals in the compacted pellet under this condition.
Proton-Vibrating within the (N-H···O) Hydrogen Bond. The inherent high conductivity and excellent thermal stability of 1 further prompted us to investigate the type of hydrogen-bonded chain and even the positions of protons. In situ SCXRD at different temperatures showed that, over a span of 300 K, there are significant variations of $a$ by $1.2\%$ for 1, corresponding to an average thermal expansion coefficient of $\alpha_{\mathrm{a}}=41.3\ \mathrm{ppm\K^{-1}}$ , which is ca. 8 and 5 times larger than those of the $b$ (4.9 ppm $\mathbf{K}^{-1})$ and $c$ $(-8.3\mathrm{ppm}\mathrm{K}^{-1})$ axes, respectively (Figure S21 and Table $S_{3}\mathrm{\dot{,}}$ ).4g,25 Carefully structural analysis supports that the anisotropic changes of unit-cell parameters arise from supramolecular interaction between 2D anion frameworks and dynamic $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ cations,25b,26 which reveal more severe vibration than that of 2D anion host frameworks (Figure $\mathbf{S}22$ and Table $S_{4}$ ). Actually, this drastically vibrating behaviour may facilitate dynamic transfer of protons along the hydrogen-bonded (N–H···O) chain.10f,10h,18
As is well known, when the included guest molecules are strongly confined within a host framework of high crystallinity,26c,27 proton dynamics within strong hydrogen bonds can be visualized at the molecular/atom level by SCXRD.26a,28 In the case of 1, the electron-density distribution of the hydrogen-bonded hydrogen atoms (H1a and H1b) of $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})^{+}$ can be clearly observed in X-ray difference Fourier maps (Figures 3a and $S_{23^{-}}S_{24}\dot{$ ). Excitingly, in situ SCXRD measurements showed that, as temperature increases, the H1a atom gradually moves away from its parent $\bf N_{1}$ atom, while the $\mathbf{N_{1}}.$ -H1b bond length remains almost unchanged (Figure $3^{\mathrm{b}}.$ ). Importantly, such peculiar behaviour is well repeated during heating-cooling cycle (Table $S5\overrightharpoon{}$ ). Besides these, considering the fact that low electron density of H atom hardly provides an accurate determination of the positions of all H1a and H1b atoms in the single-crystal of 1, a more subtle investigation of proton dynamical behaviour needs to draw support from other characterization methods, e.g. IR spectra.29 As mentioned above, the wide spectral bands $\nu_{\mathrm{{}_{1}}}$ and $\nu_{2}$ can be ascribed to the hydrogen-bonded N–H1a and $_{\mathrm{N-Hub}}$ stretching vibrations, respectively.15 When temperature rises from 298 K to $573\mathrm{K}.$ , in situ Diffuse Reflectance Infrared Fourier Transform Spectrum (DRIFTS) shows that both $\nu_{\mathrm{{r}}}$ and $\nu_{\mathrm{{r}}}$ gradually shift to higher frequencies at varying degrees (Figure $3^{\mathrm{c}},$ ), probably resulting from weakening of the hydrogen bonds at high temperatures (Table $S5,$ ),29b,30 which even overwhelms the red-shift influence caused by elongation of the N–H1a bond. Additionally, as the temperature raises, DRIFTS spectra showed that the absolute intensity of $\nu_{\mathrm{{r}}}$ gradually decreases while $\nu_{2}$ remains almost unchanged, suggesting a probable reduction in the number of H1a atoms attached to $\mathsf{N}$ centers; that is, a part of H1a atoms may shuttle between the donor $\left(\mathbf{N_{1}}\right)$ and acceptor $(\mathrm{O}_{7})$ atoms at high temperatures, although this hypothetical behaviour is not observable in X-ray difference Fourier maps. The above phenomena seem to indicate that sufficiently high temperature would eventually lead to loss of one $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ from $\mathsf{N}_{-}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ of $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{\big(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)^{+}}}\end{array}$ cations. In this way, the proton acceptor, namely a phosphate group of the host framework, can maintain the electric neutrality of 1 (Figure $\mathrm{3d}\dot{},$ ). This hypothesis has been confirmed by subsequent SCXRD, XPS, IR and luminescent measurements.
Proton Transfer from $(\mathbf{Me_{2}N H}_{2})^{+}$ Cations to $\mathbf{O}_{3}\mathbf{P}\mathbf{-}$ Groups. Since high temperature induces stretching of the N–H1a bond in $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})^{+}$ , sufficiently high energy would promote $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ transfer to the O sites of $-\mathrm{PO}_{3}$ groups. As the boiling point of $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}$ is only $7.4~^{\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{C}$ , it would easily escape from 1 after its incorporation. As expected, TGA showed that 1 gives a weight loss of $9.7\%$ from $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to 450 $^\mathrm{{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ (Figure $S_{7,}$ ), which can be attributed to $\bf{M e}_{2}\mathrm{NH}$ molecules (calcd: $9.9\%$ ) from deprotonated $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ lattice cations, finally yielding a new phase (1a), which was also verified by variable-temperature PXRD (Figures 2a and $S25$ ). Elemental analyses (EA) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) showed absence of N atoms in 1a, implying that all $\bf{M e}_{2}\mathrm{NH}$ molecules have successfully escaped from 1 (Figures 4a and S26). Another crucial information provided by XPS is that the valence state of the Eu ion in 1a is consistent with that of $\mathbf{\lambda_{1}}$ (Figure S26c). Besides, compared with 1, the two strong and wide IR spectral bands $\nu_{\mathrm{{r}}}$ and $\nu_{2}$ in 1a completely disappeared, while the $3280~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ peak referring to PO-H appeared (Figures 4b and $S_{5}\mathrm{\overline{{{\Omega}}}}$ ),31 indicating acidification of $-\mathrm{PO}_{3}$ groups and the presence of $-\mathrm{PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ after $\bf{M e}_{2}\mathrm{NH}$ removal.
A precise single-crystal structure of the final phase $\left[\mathrm{Eu(HL)}\right]$ (1a) after proton transfer from $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{\big(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)^{+}}}\end{array}$ cations to $\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{P}_{-}$ group provides the most convincing evidence. SCSC transformations are highly desirable to identify the structural changes by single-crystal X-ray crystallography.32 Taking advantage of the high thermal stability of 1a, we successfully prepared high-quality single-crystals by an extremely slow heat-treated process. Crystal data: space group $P{_{2,\left/c\right.}}$ , $a=7{\cdot}9655(5)\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $b=15.6161(9)\ \mathring{\mathcal{F}}$ , $c=$ 9.2917(6) Å, $\beta=112.201\AA(2)^{\circ}$ , $V=1070.11\AA$ (Table $S_{7}$ ). Crystal structure analysis of $\mathrm{[Eu(HL)]}$ (1a) showed that while it  retains the original space group and metal-ligand connectivity of the 2D host framework of $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})[\mathrm{Eu(L)}]$ (1), the interlayer $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}^{+}$ counter ions have been completely removed (Figures 4c and $S_{27}–S_{29}\rangle$ ) This resulted in over $-25\%$ and $-21\%$ shrinkages of the unit-cell volume and length of the $a$ -axis, respectively, together with small changes of the b $(+2.3\%)$ and $\boldsymbol{c}$ $\mathit{\Pi}_{\left\langle<\right.}^{'}$ $-1\%)$ axes (Figure S29 and Table $S_{7}\mathrm{\i}$ ). In view of the fact that removal of $\bf{M e}_{2}\mathrm{NH}$ molecules resulted in attachment of protons to O atom of phosphoric groups, SCSC transformation occurs from a cationic sandwich type MOF $(\mathrm{Me2NH_{2}})[\mathrm{Eu(HL)}]$ to a closely packed neutral one $\mathrm{[Eu(HL)]}$ thermally accompanied by proton transfer. To our best knowledge, there are few comprehensive reports on this kind of structure transformation,8c,33 even through the literature contains a large number of transformations based on studies of the initial and final structures.4g,25b,32b,34
Obviously, comparison of the proton conductivities of 1a with that of 1 can be utilized to examine the role that the hydrogen-bonded chains play when proton transfer. Nevertheless, due to poor crystallinity of the large single crystals of 1a, AC impedance measurements could not be carried out. However, measurements conducted on a compacted pellet of 1a under high humidity conditions indicate that, the proton conductivity of 1a falls sharply $\left(5.29\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}\right)$ at 100 $^\mathrm{{{o}}}C$ , $98\%$ RH), being far less than that of 1 $\left(3{\cdot}76\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}\right.$ at 100 $^{\mathrm{o}}{\cal C}_{;}$ , $98\%$ RH), which implies that the strongly hydrogen-bonded chains in 1 may facilitate transfer of protons (Figures 5a and S18). The calculated activation energy (1.50 eV) suggests that 1a requires a large number of water molecules to serve as carriers to transport protons according to the vehicle mechanism $\mathrm{(>\o.4\eV)}$ (Figure $\mathbf{5}\mathbf{b}_{\mathbf{,}}^{\mathbf{\dagger}}$ ). Therefore, a conclusion can be drawn that the hydrogen-bonded chains of 1 really play a vital role on fast proton transfer.
Reversible Proton Transfer Verified by Luminescence Properties. To better understand the role of strongly hydrogen-bonded chains (N–H···O) in the structure-property relationship of structure 1, its temperaturedependent photoluminescence properties were investigated. With increasing temperatures from 25 to ${150}^{\mathrm{~\circ}}\mathrm{C},$ , the intensity of the characteristic red emission of $\mathrm{Eu(III)}$ corresponding to the $^5\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{o}}\rightarrow^{7}\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{2}}$ transition $\left(609\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ remained steady, and then showed a slight decrease before reaching 300 ${}^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}_{:}$ , which arises from radiative relaxation of the excited-state energy induced high temperatures. Upon further heating, emission intensity gradually declined until it completely disappeared at $450^{\mathrm{~o~}}\mathrm{{C}}$ , which could not be recovered by cooling back to room temperature (Figures 6a and 6b). These observations suggest that irreversible changes happened in structural transformation from 1 to 1a. Besides this, room-temperature lifetime measurements of samples of 1 after different temperature treatments showed that the lifetime of the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{o}}$ excited state is almost unchanged $\left(\sim1.60~\mathrm{ms}\right)$ from 25 to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , but drops sharply (0.39 ms) when heated continuously (Figures 6c, $S_{30}$ and Table S8), which coincides with the process of ${\bf M e}_{2}{\bf N H}$ removal (Figures 2a, $S_{7}$ and $S25$ ). Considering that vibronic coupling with $_\mathrm{O-H}$ can effectively quench the excited state of lanthanide-based MOFs,13b,35 the present observation of luminescence quenching and shortened lifetime prompted us to conclude that formation of $_\mathrm{O-H}$ in 1a occurred after removal of deprotonated ${\mathrm{Me}}_{2}{\mathrm{NH}}$ molecules from the $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ cations.
To further verify whether the protons can reversibly hop among the acid-base pairs, experimental conversion of 1a back to 1 was carried out. When a sample of 1a was heat-treated in a $\bf{M e}_{2}\mathrm{NH}$ aqueous solution, its luminescence intensities partially recovered (Figures $\mathbf{S}_{3}\mathbf{1}$ and $S_{32}$ ). In addition, the PXRD pattern and IR of this prepared sample $(\mathbf{r})$ agreed very well with those of 1 (Figures $S_{5}$ and $S_{32}$ ). In particular, after persistent attempts, we were able to determine the single-crystal structure of $\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathbf{I}^{'}}$ (Table S9) and demonstrate its similarity to that of 1, which lent further support to the idea that $\bf{M e}_{2}\mathrm{NH}$ molecules could penetrate into the interfacial layer of 1a and finally recapture the protons from the protonated phosphate groups to form $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ cations (Figures $S_{5}$ , S31 and $S_{32}\mathrm{\overline{{.}}}$ ). In other words, reversible proton-hopping between the $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ cations and phosphate groups along the hydrogen-bonded chains has been successfully realized, which can sufficiently demonstrate the importance of continuous passageway in the hydrogen-bonding chain for proton conduction and deepens our understanding of the proton-transfer mechanism in MOFs.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, we have successfully synthesized a new acid–base pair proton-conducting MOF, which features highly ordered strongly hydrogen-bonded chains between counter cations $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ and phosphonate groups of the anionic host framework. This new crystalline material exhibits remarkable anhydrous conductivity along hydrogen-bonded (N–H···O) chains, and water-assisted proton conductivity in a powder pellet. More importantly, by virtue of the excellent single crystallinity of the material at high temperature, proton transfer mechanism was well elucidated by a series of in situ variable-temperature characterization methods, as well as controlled experiments including SCSC transformation accompanied by proton transfer between an anionic framework and an identical neutral framework. The comprehensive study demonstrated unique proton-vibrating and transfer among acid-base pairs within hydrogen-bonded (N– H···O) chains via electrostatic interactions. To our best knowledge, there exist very few reports of highly proton conductors that can provide such direct visualization of proton dynamical behaviour. We believe that this work can be instructive for further exploration of new proton conductive materials and even point the way to improvements of efficient proton sensors.
